  "final_answer": "Based on my thorough investigation, I have completed the task. Here is the summary:\n\n## Task Results: \"Poly encoder\" Title Search on ArXiv\n\nI searched for \"poly encoder\" by title on ArXiv's Advanced Search, which returned **4 articles**. I checked each article's abstract page to determine if HTML access is available.\n\n### Articles Found:\n\n| # | arXiv ID | Title | HTML Access |\n|---|----------|-------|-------------|\n| 1 | 2608.26165 | Using Poly-Encoders for Computationally Efficient Automated Creativity Assessment | ✅ Yes |\n| 2 | 2506.00968 | PolyBERT: Fine-Tuned PolyEncoder BERT-Based Model for Word Sense Disambiguation | ✅ Yes |\n| 3 | 2309.16770 | Persona-Coded Poly-Encoder: Persona-Guided Multi-Stream Conversational Sentence Scoring | ✅ Yes |\n| 4 | 1905.01969 | Poly-encoders: Transformer Architectures and Pre-training Strategies for Fast and Accurate Multi-sentence Scoring | ✅ Yes |\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n**Yes, all 4 articles in the search results provide HTML access.** Each article's abstract page lists an \"Access Paper\" section with three format options:\n- View PDF\n- **HTML (experimental)** ← available\n- TeX Source\n\nI verified the HTML access actually works by clicking the HTML (experimental) link on article arXiv:2608.26165, which successfully loaded the paper's full text in HTML format with sections including Abstract, Introduction, Methods, Results, Conclusions, and References.",
